<Project>
  <!--
    ============================================================================
    Public API baseline.
    ============================================================================

    Every public type and member of a published library is written down in
    PublicAPI.Shipped.txt and PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt beside its project.
    RS0016 fails the build on a public symbol missing from both files, and
    RS0017 fails it on a recorded symbol the code no longer declares. A change
    to the surface is then a change to a checked-in text file, which a
    reviewer sees whether or not they open the source.

    Shipped holds the surface of the last release. Unshipped holds what has
    changed since: additions as plain lines, removals as `*REMOVED*` lines.
    Cutting a release moves Unshipped into Shipped; see CONTRIBUTING.md.
    Outside that step, any edit to a Shipped file is a mistake.

    Tracked by default: every project that packs as a library. Not tracked:
    executables and `PackAsTool` projects, whose contract is a command line,
    and projects that set IsPackable=false, which ship to no feed. A project
    overrides the default by setting PeripheryTrackPublicApi itself.

    This is a .targets file rather than .props because the default reads
    IsPackable, PackAsTool and OutputType, which a csproj sets in its body.
    Directory.Build.props is imported before the body and would see them unset.
    IsPackable is still empty here for a project relying on the SDK default,
    which is set later, so the condition tests for 'false' rather than 'true'.

    RS0026 and RS0027 (optional parameters across overloads) are off. They flag
    the shape of existing overloads rather than a change to the surface.

    RS0041 (public symbol uses oblivious reference types) is off. Every project
    enables nullable and no source file disables it, so the only oblivious
    public symbols are the JsonTypeInfo properties that the net10.0
    System.Text.Json generator deliberately marks oblivious. Their lines carry
    the `~` prefix that records this.

    The same generator is why PublicAPI/<tfm>/ exists: the net8.0 generator
    annotates those properties, so their lines differ by framework. The RS0016
    code fix skips generated code and will not write them. After adding a
    [JsonSerializable] type to a public context, run
    scripts/record-generated-public-api.sh.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PeripheryTrackPublicApi)' == ''">
    <PeripheryTrackPublicApi Condition="'$(IsPackable)' != 'false' and '$(PackAsTool)' != 'true' and '$(OutputType)' != 'Exe' and '$(OutputType)' != 'WinExe'">true</PeripheryTrackPublicApi>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(PeripheryTrackPublicApi)' == 'true'">
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);RS0016;RS0017</WarningsAsErrors>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);RS0026;RS0027;RS0041</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PeripheryTrackPublicApi)' == 'true'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" Version="3.3.4" PrivateAssets="All" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The package adds the two project-level files itself; listing them here as
    well makes each a duplicate AdditionalFile, and the RS0016 code fix then
    records nothing. Only the per-framework files are added here: a symbol
    whose recorded form differs between net8.0 and net10.0 lives in
    PublicAPI/<tfm>/, which the analyzer merges with the project-level pair.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(PeripheryTrackPublicApi)' == 'true'">
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI/$(TargetFramework)/PublicAPI.Shipped.txt" Condition="Exists('PublicAPI/$(TargetFramework)/PublicAPI.Shipped.txt')" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="PublicAPI/$(TargetFramework)/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt" Condition="Exists('PublicAPI/$(TargetFramework)/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt')" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
